	<!-- what each kind of part is (2026-10-29): the Add part entry's tooltip and the text at the top of the part's form -->
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.LensPart">A lens or a mirror: a strip of the diameter between two spherical surfaces, each bulging outward, flat or hollow by the sign of its radius, the centre thickness apart. As a mirror it reflects everything; as a lens it refracts by its index, with an anti-reflection coating and absorption if wanted.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.FiberPart">A fibre: a core of higher index inside a cladding, along a smooth path of Bézier segments through its nodes, with flat or round ends. A wave coupled into the core is guided along the path; the cladding can absorb stray light at its rim.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.BraggReflectorPart">A Bragg reflector: pairs of layers of two indices stacked along the axis. It reflects strongly around the wavelength the layer thicknesses are tuned to, a quarter wave each, and passes others.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.GrinLensPart">A gradient-index lens: a rectangle whose index falls from the axis to the edge in a parabolic profile, so it focuses like a lens with flat faces.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.ZonePlatePart">A zone plate: Fresnel zones cut for a design frequency and a focal length, every second zone an absorbing block, the central zone open or, inverted, a block. It focuses by diffraction; an optional mount stops light passing outside the diameter.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.PolygonPart">A regular polygon of glass or mirror, by its number of sides and the side length.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.RectanglePart">A rectangle of glass or mirror, by length and width; linked, a square. A flat mirror is a long, narrow one.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.EllipsePart">An ellipse of glass or mirror, by the length and width of its box; linked, a circle.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.TrianglePart">A triangle of glass or mirror: the base across the axis, the apex along it, the base angles set by a preset or by hand.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.OutlinePart">A free outline of glass or mirror: a closed shape through the points you place and drag; fewer than three paint nothing.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.XorGatePart">An optical XOR gate: two input waveguides, a phase section putting the lower input a quarter period ahead, and a coupler whose upper output carries their difference. It lights when one input is lit, not both, for coherent inputs at the design frequency.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.AndGatePart">An optical AND gate: the same coupler with the lower input a quarter period behind, so the upper output carries the sum. It is brightest with both inputs lit, about four times a single one; a passive linear gate has no truer AND.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Kind.OrGatePart">An optical OR gate: the same coupler with the lower input a twelfth of a period ahead, so the upper output carries half the power of either input and of both. It lights with one input or both.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LensPart.Mirror">Mirror</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LensPart.Mirror">Make the part a mirror instead of a lens. The refractive index, the coating and the absorption are ignored then.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LensPart.RefractiveIndex">Refractive index</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LensPart.RefractiveIndex">Refractive index of the glass, 1.00 to 2.55 in steps of 0.01.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LensPart.Coating">AR coating</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LensPart.Coating">Thickness in pixels of the anti-reflection band just inside the outline; 0 for none. A quarter-wave layer is λ / (4 √n) pixels thick, λ being the wavelength the source form shows; a graded band is the better the thicker, from a wavelength on.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LensPart.Absorption">Absorption</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LensPart.Absorption">How strongly the part absorbs waves, 0 (not at all) to 255, as the scene image's blue channel. It rises from nothing at the outline over the absorption ramp, so a wave enters the part instead of being reflected. A mirror ignores it.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LensPart.AbsorptionRamp">Absorption ramp</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LensPart.AbsorptionRamp">Depth in pixels over which the absorption rises from nothing at the outline to its full value; the longer, the less of a wave is reflected. A part thinner than twice the ramp reaches its full absorption in its middle.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LensPart.Diameter">Diameter</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LensPart.Diameter">Extent across the axis in pixels.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LensPart.IsConcave">Concave (diverging)</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LensPart.IsConcave">Whether the lens diverges, its surfaces curving inward. Ticking it turns the lens around by flipping the sign of both radii; a flat surface stays flat.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LensPart.R1">R1</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LensPart.R1">Radius of the first surface in pixels: positive bulges outward, negative is concave, 0 is flat. A radius too curved for the diameter and the centre thickness is raised to the smallest that fits.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LensPart.R2">R2</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LensPart.R2">Radius of the second surface in pixels: negative bulges outward, positive is concave, 0 is flat. A radius too curved for the diameter and the centre thickness is raised to the smallest that fits.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LensPart.Thickness">Centre thickness</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LensPart.Thickness">Distance between the two surfaces on the axis in pixels.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.ShapePart.Mirror">Mirror</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.ShapePart.Mirror">Make the part a mirror instead of glass. The refractive index, the coating and the absorption are ignored then.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.ShapePart.RefractiveIndex">Refractive index</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.ShapePart.RefractiveIndex">Refractive index of the glass, 1.00 to 2.55 in steps of 0.01.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.ShapePart.Coating">AR coating</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.ShapePart.Coating">Thickness in pixels of the anti-reflection band just inside the outline; 0 for none. A quarter-wave layer is λ / (4 √n) pixels thick, λ being the wavelength the source form shows; a graded band is the better the thicker, from a wavelength on.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.CoatedPart.CoatingProfile">Coating profile</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.CoatedPart.CoatingProfile">How the index runs through the band: a layer at √n, the quarter-wave idea, or a graded rise from the medium outside to the glass. A graded band reflects less than a layer once it is a wavelength thick or more, at any wavelength; a layer only at the wavelength it is cut for.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.CoatedPart.FrontCoated">Coat the front</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.CoatedPart.FrontCoated">Put the band on the front: the faces turned toward a source on the left, a lens's first surface. Clear it to leave them bare, as the inner faces of a cavity are.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.CoatedPart.BackCoated">Coat the back</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.CoatedPart.BackCoated">Put the band on the back: the faces turned away from a source on the left, a lens's second surface. Clear it to leave them bare.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.CoatedPart.SidesCoated">Coat the sides</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.CoatedPart.SidesCoated">Put the band on the sides: the faces that are neither front nor back, a lens's rim, a slab's narrow ends. Clear it to leave them bare.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.ShapePart.Absorption">Absorption</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.ShapePart.Absorption">How strongly the part absorbs waves, 0 (not at all) to 255, as the scene image's blue channel. It rises from nothing at the outline over the absorption ramp, so a wave enters the part instead of being reflected. A mirror ignores it.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.ShapePart.AbsorptionRamp">Absorption ramp</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.ShapePart.AbsorptionRamp">Depth in pixels over which the absorption rises from nothing at the outline to its full value; the longer, the less of a wave is reflected. A part thinner than twice the ramp reaches its full absorption in its middle.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.PolygonPart.Sides">Number of sides</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.PolygonPart.Sides">Number of sides, 3 to 64.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.PolygonPart.Side">Side length</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.PolygonPart.Side">Length of each side in pixels.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.RectanglePart.Length">Length</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.RectanglePart.Length">Extent along the axis in pixels.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.RectanglePart.Width">Width</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.RectanglePart.Width">Extent across the axis in pixels.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.RectanglePart.Linked">Linked</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.RectanglePart.Linked">Keep the width equal to the length: a square.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.EllipsePart.Length">Length</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.EllipsePart.Length">Diameter along the axis in pixels.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.EllipsePart.Width">Width</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.EllipsePart.Width">Diameter across the axis in pixels.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.EllipsePart.Linked">Linked</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.EllipsePart.Linked">Keep the width equal to the length: a circle.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.TrianglePart.Base">Base</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.TrianglePart.Base">Length of the base in pixels; the base lies across the axis, toward a source on the left.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.TrianglePart.Type">Type</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.TrianglePart.Type">Equilateral, right isosceles (the 45° prism), 30-60-90, isosceles with one typed angle, or custom with both typed.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.TrianglePart.AngleA">Angle A</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.TrianglePart.AngleA">Base angle at the first end of the base, in degrees. Typed for the isosceles and custom types; the presets fix it.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.TrianglePart.AngleB">Angle B</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.TrianglePart.AngleB">Base angle at the second end of the base, in degrees. Typed for the custom type; the others fix it.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.OutlinePart.Points">Points</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.OutlinePart.Points">The outline's vertices relative to the centre, before rotation. Fewer than three points paint nothing.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberPart.CoreIndex">Core index</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberPart.CoreIndex">Refractive index of the core, 1.00 to 2.55 in steps of 0.01.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberPart.CoreDiameter">Core diameter</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberPart.CoreDiameter">Diameter of the core in pixels.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberPart.CladdingIndex">Cladding index</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberPart.CladdingIndex">Refractive index of the cladding, 1.00 to 2.55 in steps of 0.01. Below the core's index, the fibre guides.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberPart.CladdingThickness">Cladding thickness</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberPart.CladdingThickness">Thickness of the cladding around the core in pixels; 0 for a bare core.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberPart.Coating">AR coating</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberPart.Coating">Thickness in pixels of the anti-reflection band just inside the outer boundary and the end faces; 0 for none. A quarter-wave layer is λ / (4 √n) pixels thick, λ being the wavelength the source form shows; a graded band is the better the thicker, from a wavelength on.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberPart.Absorption">Cladding absorption</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberPart.Absorption">Peak absorption of the cladding's absorbing band, 0 (not at all) to 255, as the scene image's blue channel: a hump in the outer part of the cladding, rising over the band width from nothing, peaking, and falling to nothing at the outer edge, so the guided wave's tail stays untouched and stray light is absorbed from either side. Nothing on a bare core.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberPart.AbsorptionRamp">Absorption band</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberPart.AbsorptionRamp">Half the absorbing band's width in pixels: the absorption rises over this many pixels to its peak and falls over as many to the outer edge. A band wider than the cladding is squeezed into it.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberPart.Ends">Ends</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberPart.Ends">How the two ends are cut: flat, straight across the path like a cleaved fibre, or rounded with the outer radius. The AR coating follows a flat end face.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberPart.Nodes">Nodes</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberPart.Nodes">The path's nodes relative to the centre, before rotation, each with the handles of the cubic segments meeting there. Fewer than two nodes paint nothing.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberNode.X">X</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberNode.X">X relative to the part's centre in canvas pixels, before rotation.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberNode.Y">Y</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberNode.Y">Y relative to the part's centre in canvas pixels, before rotation.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberNode.InX">In X</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberNode.InX">X of the in-handle relative to the node, in canvas pixels before rotation. It shapes the segment arriving from the previous node; zero makes that segment straight at this end.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberNode.InY">In Y</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberNode.InY">Y of the in-handle relative to the node, in canvas pixels before rotation.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberNode.OutX">Out X</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberNode.OutX">X of the out-handle relative to the node, in canvas pixels before rotation. It shapes the segment leaving to the next node; zero makes that segment straight at this end.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberNode.OutY">Out Y</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberNode.OutY">Y of the out-handle relative to the node, in canvas pixels before rotation.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.FiberNode.Smooth">Smooth</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.FiberNode.Smooth">Keep the two handles opposite each other through the node, so the path runs smoothly through it. Switching it on lines them up at once; afterwards moving or typing one handle turns the other with it, keeping its length. Off, the node may be a corner.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.BraggReflectorPart.Pairs">Layer pairs</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.BraggReflectorPart.Pairs">Number of layer pairs, 1 to 200.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.BraggReflectorPart.Thickness1">Thickness 1</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.BraggReflectorPart.Thickness1">Thickness of the first layer of each pair in pixels. A quarter-wave layer is λ / (4 n) pixels thick.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.BraggReflectorPart.Thickness2">Thickness 2</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.BraggReflectorPart.Thickness2">Thickness of the second layer of each pair in pixels. A quarter-wave layer is λ / (4 n) pixels thick.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.BraggReflectorPart.Index1">Index 1</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.BraggReflectorPart.Index1">Refractive index of the first layer of each pair, 1.00 to 2.55 in steps of 0.01.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.BraggReflectorPart.Index2">Index 2</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.BraggReflectorPart.Index2">Refractive index of the second layer of each pair, 1.00 to 2.55 in steps of 0.01.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.BraggReflectorPart.Width">Width</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.BraggReflectorPart.Width">Extent of the stack across the axis in pixels.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.GrinLensPart.Length">Length</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.GrinLensPart.Length">Extent along the axis in pixels.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.GrinLensPart.Diameter">Diameter</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.GrinLensPart.Diameter">Extent across the axis in pixels.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.GrinLensPart.CentreIndex">Centre index</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.GrinLensPart.CentreIndex">Refractive index on the axis, 1.00 to 2.55 in steps of 0.01.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.GrinLensPart.EdgeIndex">Edge index</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.GrinLensPart.EdgeIndex">Refractive index at the edge, 1.00 to 2.55 in steps of 0.01. The profile between the axis and the edge is parabolic.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.GrinLensPart.Coating">AR coating</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.GrinLensPart.Coating">Thickness in pixels of the anti-reflection band just inside the outline; 0 for none. A quarter-wave layer is λ / (4 √n) pixels thick, λ being the wavelength the source form shows; a graded band is the better the thicker, from a wavelength on.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LogicGatePart.DesignFrequency">Design frequency</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LogicGatePart.DesignFrequency">The source frequency the gate is designed for, on the same scale as a source's frequency: the frequency of the sources that drive it. The wavelength, and from it the core width, the coupler and the phase section, follow from it, so the gate works at this frequency only.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LogicGatePart.Coating">AR coating</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LogicGatePart.Coating">Thickness in pixels of the anti-reflection band at the ends ticked below, with the index √n of the core and of the cladding; 0 for none. A quarter-wave band, the least reflecting, is λ / (4 √n) pixels thick; the form shows it for the core.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LogicGatePart.InputCoated">AR on input side</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LogicGatePart.InputCoated">Put the anti-reflection band on the input end. Switch it off where the gate takes its light from a gate before it, so the wave passes through the junction.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LogicGatePart.OutputCoated">AR on output side</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LogicGatePart.OutputCoated">Put the anti-reflection band on the output end. Switch it off where the gate hands its light to a gate after it, so the wave passes through the junction.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LogicGatePart.UpperOutputActive">Upper output</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LogicGatePart.UpperOutputActive">Let the upper output's light leave the gate. Switched off, an absorber with a soft rise takes it in the last stretch of the arm.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.LogicGatePart.LowerOutputActive">Lower output</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.LogicGatePart.LowerOutputActive">Let the lower output's light leave the gate. Switched off, an absorber with a soft rise takes it in the last stretch of the arm.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.ZonePlatePart.DesignFrequency">Target frequency</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.ZonePlatePart.DesignFrequency">The source frequency the plate is designed for, on the same scale as a source&apos;s frequency. The zones follow from the wavelength it gives, so the plate focuses this frequency at the focal length; other frequencies focus elsewhere.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.ZonePlatePart.FocalLength">Focal length</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.ZonePlatePart.FocalLength">Distance in pixels from the plate to its focus, for a plane wave arriving along the axis.</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Label.ZonePlatePart.Inverse">Inverse</s:String>
	<s:String x:Key="Hint.ZonePlatePart.Inverse">Exchange the zones: the central zone becomes a block, the first ring open, and so on. The focus stays where it is.</s:String>
